From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nabeel Sowan <nabeel@vibes.se>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 18:28:52 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] tdm: classify the user's session as a user session, not a
 greeter

tdm has been registering every logged-in session with logind as class
"greeter". XDG_SESSION_CLASS=greeter is put into the PAM environment by
doPAMAuth(), which authenticates the person logging in, and the same PAM
handle is carried through to pam_open_session() with nothing resetting it
in between. There is exactly one pam_start() and one pam_open_session() in
the backend, and the greeter has no PAM session of its own, so the only
session that value ever reached was the user's.

The visible cost is that such a session is not lockable. logind derives
CanLock from the class at session creation and reports false for a
greeter, and CanLock is a const property, so nothing can correct it later:
loginctl lock-session, the Lock and Unlock signals and SetLockedHint are
all inert for the logged-in user. Measured on a TDE 14.1.6 desktop, where
the session shows Service=tdm-trinity, Type=x11, Class=greeter and
CanLock=false.

systemd gates this on the class directly, in src/login/logind-session.h:

    /* Which session classes have a lock screen concept? */
    #define SESSION_CLASS_CAN_LOCK(class) \
            (IN_SET((class), SESSION_USER, SESSION_USER_EARLY))

SESSION_GREETER is excluded there and in SESSION_CLASS_CAN_STOP_ON_IDLE,
and those two are the whole difference: CAN_IDLE, CAN_DISPLAY,
CAN_TAKE_DEVICE and CAN_CHANGE_TYPE all admit a greeter already. So this
restores lockability and stop-on-idle, and changes nothing else.

It cannot ever have worked as intended. pam_systemd acts in the session
phase rather than the authentication phase, so a class set before
pam_authenticate() has no effect until a session is opened, and the only
session opened here belongs to the user.

Set it where the code already observes that the greeter has gone, which is
immediately before the session is opened, and read the result of
pam_putenv() rather than discarding it as the original line did.

This changes what logind is told about the session. It does not change who
may authenticate, and it grants no new rights: a session classified as a
user session is what an ordinary login already is everywhere else.
---
 tdm/backend/client.c | 14 +++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/tdm/backend/client.c b/tdm/backend/client.c
index 5a699738e..d3f6049b0 100644
--- a/tdm/backend/client.c
+++ b/tdm/backend/client.c
@@ -396,7 +396,6 @@ doPAMAuth( const char *psrv, struct pam_data *pdata )
 # ifdef PAM_FAIL_DELAY
 	pam_set_item( pamh, PAM_FAIL_DELAY, (void *)fail_delay );
 # endif
-	pretc = pam_putenv( pamh, "XDG_SESSION_CLASS=greeter" );
 	ReInitErrorLog();
 
 	Debug( " pam_authenticate() ...\n" );
@@ -1377,6 +1376,19 @@ StartClient()
 
 #ifdef USE_PAM
 	/* the greeter is gone by now ... */
+
+	/*
+	 * ... so the session about to be opened is the user's, and it must be
+	 * classified as such. logind derives a session's class from this at
+	 * pam_open_session() time, and a session it believes to be a login
+	 * greeter is not lockable: CanLock stays false, which is a const
+	 * property fixed at session creation, so loginctl lock-session and the
+	 * Lock and Unlock signals never work for the logged-in user.
+	 */
+	if ((pretc = pam_putenv( pamh, "XDG_SESSION_CLASS=user" )) != PAM_SUCCESS)
+		LogError( "pam_putenv(XDG_SESSION_CLASS) failed: %s\n",
+		          pam_strerror( pamh, pretc ) );
+
 	pconv.conv = PAM_conv_null;
 	pconv.appdata_ptr = 0;
 	if ((pretc = pam_set_item( pamh, PAM_CONV, &pconv )) != PAM_SUCCESS) {
